    longDescription:
      "WiFiTrail tells you what your device's connection actually is, and measures how fast it actually runs.\n\nCONNECTION\nThe Connection tab reports the transport your device is using, straight from the operating system: Wi-Fi, cellular, ethernet, VPN, or several at once. On Wi-Fi it shows the network name and access point where the system releases them, and lists the IPv4 and IPv6 addresses this device holds on its own interfaces. Where the system withholds the network name, WiFiTrail says so and explains why instead of showing a placeholder. Refresh re-reads the OS, and the screen also updates itself when the connection changes.\n\nSPEED TEST\nThe speed test transfers real bytes over HTTPS to and from Cloudflare's measurement endpoint. It reports download, upload, median ping and jitter, and prints the megabytes moved and the seconds taken underneath each figure so you can check the arithmetic. Before the first run every reading is a dash and the caption says it has not been measured. If a run is cancelled, times out, or fails, it ends with the reason and no number at all.\n\nSECURITY\nFive pieces of general Wi-Fi guidance, labelled as advice rather than a scan. WiFiTrail gives your network no score and no pass or fail, because it cannot inspect your router, firewall, DNS or open ports.\n\nWHAT IT CANNOT DO\nNo app on iOS or Android is allowed to list the other networks around you, so WiFiTrail does not offer that, and it says so on screen rather than leaving an empty list. It reports no ISP, gateway, DNS or channel, because a sandboxed app cannot obtain them.\n\nPERMISSIONS AND PRIVACY\nLocation is requested for one reason: the system releases the Wi-Fi network's name to an app only once that permission is granted. WiFiTrail takes no location fix, stores no location and sends none. There is no account, no sign-in, no ads and no analytics, and nothing leaves your device except the speed test's own traffic. Deny location and everything else still works.\n\nLANGUAGES\nThe interface is translated into twelve languages: English, Arabic, Chinese, French, German, Hindi, Italian, Japanese, Korean, Portuguese, Russian and Spanish. It follows your device language, and a picker in the app overrides it.",
